The number of available lipases (triacylglycerol hydrolases; EC 3.1.1.3) has increased considerably since the 1980s. This is mainly a result of the enormous achievements made in the cloning and expression of enzymes from microorganisms, as well as of an increasing demand for these biocatalysts. Before the mid-1980s, lipases were used most frequently in laundry applications and in the modification of triglycerides. However, extensive research proved that they are also very effective biocatalysts for the synthesis of optically pure compounds. Moreover, they often exhibit very good stability, are active in a wide range of organic solvents and do not need cofactors. It is possible that $>1000$ different substances have now been resolved efficiently using lipases [1]. In addition, several industrial processes based on lipase catalysis have been established [2]. In terms of product value, the large-scale process $(>2000$ tonnes year−1) operated by BASF (Ludwigshafen, Germany) for the kinetic resolution of various amines [3] probably represents the most striking example of industrial success.
However, the performance of an enzyme that is active in one given reaction is not always sufficient for its application in an industrial process. Consequently, various strategies are run in parallel to discover the principal suitability of a given lipase (Fig. 1). These strategies are not restricted to lipases and are, in principle, applicable to other biocatalysts [4]. However, they have been studied in some detail in lipases and are therefore much more established with lipases than with other enzymes that have come into focus more recently, and which are still to be turned into industrially useful biocatalysts. In principle, these approaches can be divided into three major categories (Box 1).
This article emphasizes recent developments in this field. Detailed information about lipases in general, their properties and application in biocatalysis can be found in several reviews [5,6] and books [1,7,8].
## A brief overview about lipases
Lipases are a versatile group of biocatalysts. Their natural function is the hydrolysis of triglycerides to partial glycerides and fatty acids during digestion. Unlike carboxyl esterases (EC 3.1.1.1) [9], lipase catalysis occurs at the lipid–water interface and most lipases show the phenomenon of a so-called interfacial activation – high catalytic activity is observed only in the presence of a hydrophobic phase, a lipid droplet dispersed in water or an organic solvent. This phenomenon has been related to the presence of a hydrophobic oligopeptide (the lid or flap) covering the entrance to the active site. In a hydrophobic environment, the lid moves aside and the substrate can enter the binding pocket. Exceptions to interfacial activation are lipases from Pseudomonas glumae, Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Candida antarctica B but all of these lipases contain a (smaller) lid [5,10]. In all lipases, a catalytic triad of serine, histidine and aspartate (or glutamate in a few lipases, e.g. those from Geotrichum candidum or Candida rugosa) is present (Fig. 2). The tetrahedral intermediate formed during the transition state is also stabilized by an oxyanion hole. All lipases show a characteristic $\upalpha/\upbeta$ -hydrolase fold, which is also found in esterases, proteases and haloperoxidases [11]. They are active over a broad range of pH and temperature and most microbial lipases show maximum activity at $\mathrm{pH}7-9$ and temperatures of $30{\mathrm{-}}40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
The same holds true for esterases, which are distinguished from lipases by the lack of interfacial activation and differences in the substrate spectra. Esterases do not hydrolyse long-chain fatty acid esters and prefer water-soluble substrates.
Some lipases, especially those from Rhizopus spp., also exhibit phospholipase ${\bf A}_{1}$ activity and can therefore be used to synthesize 1-lyso-phospholipids by the hydrolysis of phospholipids. Because of acyl migration, the fatty acid located at sn2 can migrate and thus the 2-lyso phospholipids can also form. This principle can be used for the degumming of oil.
The Lipase Engineering Database (LED; http://www.led.uni-stuttgart.de) provides information on sequence–structure–function relationships of 92 microbial lipases and homologous serine hydrolases assigned into 15 superfamilies and 32 homologous families [12]. This database provides rapid and up-to-date access to a plethora of structural and other relevant biochemical data of lipases, with special emphasis on examples from protein engineering.
The production of lipases is increasingly achieved by expression in recombinant organisms, for instance lipase from Rhizomucor miehei can be produced in the fungi Aspergillus oryzae [13] and a considerable number of other lipase genes has been cloned and functionally expressed [14]. The use of suitable expression systems, which often enable secretion of mature lipases into the cultivation medium, has made the effective production of large amounts of active lipase possible, as was shown for an isoenzyme of a lipase from Candida rugosa [15].
The main interest in their application stems from the high activity and stability of lipases in non-aqueous systems (organic solvents but also supercritical fluids), an extremely broad substrate range and high stereoselectivity. Given that they are active in the presence of low amounts of water, reverse reactions of ester hydrolysis (esterification or transesterification) are possible and therefore the range of reactions that can be catalyzed is considerably broadened.
