The solubilization of lipases for reactions in organic solvents was studied by various groups in the 1980s and early 1990s [1,25,26].Although it could be demonstrated that these modified biocatalysts occasionally show significantly higher activity than dispersed or immobilized lipases, their isolation proved difficult and no general method for solubilization in different solvents is available. No major achievements have been reported recently for this area of research.
## Reactionengine ringmethods
The use of reaction engineering can certainly improve the performance of lipases for a given application. However, in the vast majority of examples, the identification of best reaction conditions is still a tedious game of trial and error. The use of different acyl donors in lipase-catalyzed (trans-) esterification reactions is well established and enol esters such as vinyl acetate are frequently used because they allow an irreversible and rapid reaction. The logP concept [27] is often used to select appropriate solvents but the influence of the solvent on activity, stability and enantioselectivity is still rather unpredictable.
The most recent achievement was the discovery that enzyme-catalyzed reactions can be performed in ionic liquids (ILs). The most commonly used ILs are 1-butyl-3-methylimidazolium tetrafluoroborate $\mathrm{([BMM]BF_{4}}$ ) and the hexafluorophosphate $(\mathrm{[BMM]PF_{6}})$ . Furthermore, the solvation properties of ILs can be modulated using different cations or anions, resulting in water-miscible and water-immiscible systems [28].
The first report of the successful use of these solvents found the performance of Candida antarctica lipase B (CAL-B) to be similar to that of conventional organic solvents in alcoholysis, ammoniolysis and perhydrolysis [29]. It was shown that the enantioselectivity of CAL-B and Pseudomonas cepacia lipase (PCL) could be maintained, or even substantially enhanced, in the presence of an IL in the resolution of several secondary alcohols [30–32]. The Kazlauskas group also reported that the acetylation of glucose in ILs is much more selective, because a subsequent acetylation of 6-O-acetyl glucose, which occurs in conventional solvents, could be drastically reduced [32]. However, it must be emphasized that these observations are not the result of modifications of the biocatalysts by the ILs but of changes in the polarity of the solvent system and, presumably, of ionic effects.
Another often-studied parameter for lipase-catalyzed reactions in organic media is the water activity $(\mathbf{a}_{\mathrm{w}})$ [33]. Water activity not only has an important role in maintaining the catalytic activity of an enzyme in a given solvent but also it has a strong influence on acyl migration. Thus high product yields can be obtained only when water activity is subject to careful control, for example in the synthesis of structured triglycerides [34] and phospholipids. For instance, the yield in the lipase-catalyzed synthesis of phospholipids by fatty acid exchange at the sn1-positions was substantially enhanced by careful control of water activity [35].
## Molecular biology methods
The abovementioned methods depend on the availability of the biocatalyst to be optimized for a certain application. Until the mid-1990s, only a very limited number of lipases was available for biocatalysis. However, since that time efficient strategies have been developed to expand the range of accessible and improved lipases (as well as other biocatalysts). These strategies include (1) the exploration of biodiversity to expand the sources and number of new biocatalysts; (2) the use of rational protein design to improve enzymes for which the 3D structure has been elucidated or homology-modelled; and (3) directed evolution. Consequently, many more lipases are now available (i.e. in the form of test-kits) and even more biocatalysts are under development.
## Rationalproteindesign
Crystallographers have solved many lipase structures and paved the way for the use of computer modelling to rationalize the substrate specificity and enantioselectivity of these enzymes [36]. Various
approaches to understanding the origin of the enantioselectivity of lipases and to provide guidelines for improving activity and selectivity by rational design have been described [37–39]. In an impressive example, the enantioselectivity $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{E})$ of the C. antarctica B-lipasecatalyzed resolution of 1-chloro-2-octanol was improved from ${\mathrm{E}}=14$ to $\mathrm{E}{=}28$ by the exchange of a single amino acid (Ser47Ala), as predicted by molecular modelling [40]. In another approach [41], a transition-state stabilizing threonine near the active site was replaced by valine using site-directed mutagenesis. This resulted in a loss of lipase activity. Interestingly, this could be restored by using 2-hydroxy-propanoate as the substrate (because it provides the ‘missing’hydroxyl group). This also resulted in an improved enantioselectivity (from ${\mathrm{E}}{=}1.6$ (WT) to 22 $\mathrm{(Thr40Val)},$ . Other recent examples include the development of models to understand the stereoselectivity of lipase from Pseudomonas cepacia towards secondary alcohols [42] and $\gamma-$ and δ-lactones [43].
According to Pleiss and co-workers [12], lipases differ in a certain motif, located in the active site, which is responsible for the stabilization of the tetrahedral intermediate during catalysis. This motif is called the GX or GGGX motif (in single-letter amino acid code and in which X denotes any amino acid), and is located in the oxyanion binding pocket. So far, the motif has been described only in a phenomenological way. Some very recent research has shown that this motif also controls the activity of lipases towards tertiary alcohols – an important class of compounds that have so far been accepted only by lipase from C. rugosa (CRL) and lipaseA from C. antarctica (CAL-A). CAL-A is a true exception within the lipase families because it presumably does not contain the typical $\upalpha/\upbeta.$ -hydrolase fold and its structure is unknown. Indeed, not only is there experimental evidence that CRL (which is known to contain the GGGX motif) acts on acetates of tertiary alcohols but also that a considerable number of other GGGX-motif hydrolases (three acetylcholine esterases, a pig liver esterase and a $p$ -nitrobenzyl esterase from Bacillus subtilis, BsubpNBE) hydrolysed four model acetates [44], albeit at moderate enantioselectivity $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{E}=1.5\mathbf{-}11,}\end{array}$ ). Based on a thorough computer-modelling, mutants were predicted for BsubpNBE that show at least a sixfold improved enantioselectivity (from $\mathbf{E}=6$ ( WT) to $\scriptstyle\mathbf{E}=19$ (Gly105Ala)) in the resolution of $(R,S)$ -2-phenyl-3- butin-2-yl acetate. GX-motif hydrolases were all inactive towards the model compounds.
## Directedevolution
Directed evolution combines random mutagenesis with screening or selection for the improved properties. This approach has major advantages over the traditional methods such as the use of UV light or chemical mutagens because the nucleotide sequences subjected to random mutagenesis can be restricted to the gene encoding the protein of interest, or even to parts of it, instead of targeting a complete organism. The most
prominent methods are error-prone PCR [45] and DNA shuffling [46]. Experimental details for these and other methods that can be used to create mutant libraries (and many other applications) can be found in the literature [47–50]. The major challenge in directed evolution is the availability of suitable systems for the identification of desired mutants out of a library of $10^{3}–10^{8}$ variants. This is performed either by selection (i.e. by a growth-related assay) or by screening using high-throughput technologies. Various sophisticated screening systems have been developed for lipases and esterases [51–55] and these allow the rapid identification of active and stereoselective biocatalysts in a microtiter plate format.
Recent examples of the application of directed evolution to improve lipase and esterases include the alteration of the substrate specificity of Pseudomonas fluorescens esterase (PFE) [56]. In addition, the enantioselectivity of PFE was increased from $\mathbf{E}{=}3.5$ to ${\mathrm{E}}=5.2{-6.6}$ in a single round of directed evolution [57], and further increased to $\mathbf{E}=12$ by saturation mutagenesis.
gave E values of ${>}50$ [60]. In addition, mutants were identified as having opposite stereopreference.
Several 1,3-regiospecific lipases also show phospholipase ${\bf A}_{1}$ activity and thus cleave the primary ester bond in phospholipids. Directed evolution has been shown to be an efficient tool for improving the phospholipase ${\bf A}_{1}$ activity of lipase from Bacillus thermocatenulatus [61] and from Staphylococcus hyicus [62].
Despite these convincing examples, many factors determining lipase properties are not yet well understood and predicting positive mutations, especially for properties such as $\mathrm{\bfpH}$ , temperature and solvent stability, is still a difficult task. However, information derived from directed evolution approaches will certainly contribute to the understanding of structure–function relationships.
